 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 47,
   "y": 43,
   "text": "MIND THE GAP. The phrase was first recorded in 1969, between a train and a platform on the Northern line. The voice you hear at one station belongs to an actor whose widow still visits to hear it. A very London love story."
  },
  {
   "x": 44,
   "y": 36,
   "text": "CENTRAL LINE. Opened 1900 as the 'Twopenny Tube', because every ride cost twopence. Today you can't buy a Tube sandwich for twopence, or twopence for a Tube sandwich."
  },
  {
   "x": 36,
   "y": 32,
   "text": "THE DEEP TUNNELS. The London Underground runs for 250 miles, and some of it is much deeper than it looks. Down here is less 'sky' and more 'maze'."
  },
  {
   "x": 19,
   "y": 14,
   "text": "NORTHERN LINE. The deepest station in the system is Hampstead, 58.5 metres down. Its lift ride is a good chance to look at your shoes."
  },
  {
   "x": 55,
   "y": 11,
   "text": "DISUSED STATION. London has about 40 ghost stations, closed for decades and left like time capsules. Their old posters still advertise toothpaste, Bovril, and a variety show of 1938."
  },
  {
   "x": 20,
   "y": 5,
   "text": "TO THE VANE TOWER. Staff only. Persons without a lanyard will be asked to take a seat. There are no seats. This is the Enclosure's idea of a joke."
  }
 ],
